cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  tmtcdiff: a change-class taxonomy for telemetry and command dictionaries,
  with silent-semantic-change ranking and a computed coverage verdict
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-29
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Compares two versions of a telemetry and command dictionary and reports,
  against an optional corpus of procedures, scripts and configuration files,
  which existing references break and which now mean something else. The
  contribution is not the structural comparison, which general structured
  differs already do well; it is a 21-class change taxonomy for telemetry
  definitions, partitioned into three tiers by failure visibility, and the
  ranking of the silent tier above loud breakage. A silent change leaves every
  existing reference resolving and running while altering what it means: a
  changed scale factor, unit, bit offset, byte order, data type, enumeration
  mapping or command argument position under an unchanged name. A loud change
  stops a reference resolving and announces itself. Eleven of the twenty-one
  classes are silent, seven loud and three advisory, and the silent tier is
  reported first by every function in the package. Validation is level 2 and
  every number is measured against a seeded synthetic generator; no flight
  database is used, read or approximated. Per-class detection measured
  precision 1.0000 and recall 1.0000 over 420 injected changes when one class
  is injected per pair, and micro-averaged precision 0.9665 and recall 0.9619
  when all twenty-one are injected into the same pair, where command_removed
  recall falls to 0.7000 and command_renamed precision to 0.7692 because an
  unrelated removal and an unrelated addition can be paired as a rename.
  Rename detection is inferred rather than read, so its false-match rate was
  measured across a swept grid of twelve thresholds, five dictionary sizes,
  twelve seeds and two matching strategies; it falls from 0.2258 to exactly
  zero at a threshold of 0.80, which is the value a criterion declared before
  the sweep then selected, while recall holds at 1.0000 to 0.85 and falls to
  0.7208 at 0.95. The greedy baseline matched the optimal rectangular
  assignment exactly at the selected threshold, 924 correct and 0 false for
  both, and never differed by more than two false matches in any cell of the
  grid; the optimal matcher is kept as the default because it cannot be worse
  by construction, not because it measured better. The required negative
  control holds: a document reordered and respelled across its entries, keys,
  enumerations and argument declarations differs on 2526 text lines at sixty
  parameters and produces an empty semantic diff on all twenty-four swept
  pairs, while sixty-six of sixty-six injected silent changes are still
  detected through the same reordering. Five bounds and identities derived
  from mathematics the diff does not use hold on every pair. One tier
  assignment is reported as an assumption rather than a measurement:
  argument_added is classified loud on the assumption that a command system
  rejects a call missing an argument, and the package cannot confirm that. The
  impact assessment returns a coverage verdict as a first-class value naming
  what it could not determine - references constructed at run time, dictionary
  items the corpus never mentions, sources that could not be read, rename
  candidates on the threshold, and fields the model does not represent - and
  an empty finding list is never rendered as a statement that an upgrade is
  safe. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for
  operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - telemetry
  - telecommand
  - spacecraft-operations
  - semantic-diff
  - change-impact-analysis
  - mission-database
  - regression-detection
references:
  - type: standard
    title: "XML Telemetric and Command Exchange (XTCE)"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 660.0-B"
    notes: >-
      The source of the modelling convention this package's record layout
      follows: a telemetry parameter is located by a bit offset and a bit
      width inside a container, carries a calibrator, and may carry an
      enumerated parameter type. This package implements none of the XTCE
      schema, reads none of its files, and takes no numerical value from it.
  - type: standard
    title: "Telemetry and telecommand packet utilization"
    institution:
      name: "European Cooperation for Space Standardization"
    number: "ECSS-E-ST-70-41C"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      The degree-one polynomial calibration and the status-code calibration
      that the scale, offset and enum fields model, and the out-of-limit
      checking service that makes a limits change a distinct class from a
      calibration change. No numerical value in this repository is taken from
      it.
  - type: standard
    title: "Space Packet Protocol"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 133.0-B-2"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      The packet structure the bit_offset and bit_width fields locate a
      parameter within. Named for context; this package parses no packets.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ratcliff
        given-names: "J. W."
      - family-names: Metzener
        given-names: "D. E."
    title: "Pattern Matching: the Gestalt Approach"
    journal: "Dr. Dobb's Journal"
    year: 1988
    notes: >-
      The similarity measure behind difflib.SequenceMatcher.ratio, which
      supplies the name and description terms of the rename score.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Kuhn
        given-names: "H. W."
    title: "The Hungarian method for the assignment problem"
    journal: "Naval Research Logistics Quarterly"
    volume: 2
    year: 1955
    notes: >-
      The assignment problem the optimal rename matcher solves over the
      removed-by-added score matrix.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Crouse
        given-names: "D. F."
    title: "On implementing 2D rectangular assignment algorithms"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Aerospace and Electronic Systems"
    volume: 52
    issue: 4
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      The algorithm scipy documents as the implementation behind
      scipy.optimize.linear_sum_assignment, used for the optimal pairing.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Feller
        given-names: "W."
    title: "An Introduction to Probability Theory and Its Applications, Volume I"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1968
    notes: >-
      Chapter II, the occupancy counting argument behind
      signature_collision_bound, the bound the measured false-match rate is
      checked against.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    notes: >-
      The exact binomial interval reported beside every measured rate. Chosen
      because the counts of interest here are small and often zero, where a
      normal approximation gives an interval of zero width.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: van Rijsbergen
        given-names: "C. J."
    title: "Information Retrieval"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Butterworths"
    year: 1979
    notes: >-
      Chapter 7, the precision, recall and F1 definitions used throughout the
      per-class detection tables.
